</funding-source><award-id>No. APICHU RBFC 2016</award-id></award-group><funding-statement>This work was supported by the Region Bourgogne Franche-Comté (No. APICHU RBFC 2016). The China Scholarship Council supported the research and work of Yaoyao Xie (No. 202209510006).</funding-statement></funding-group></support-group><counts><fig-count count="7"/><table-count count="0"/><equation-count count="0"/><ref-count count="50"/><page-count count="20"/><word-count count="7258"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-live</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-embargo</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-released</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-open-access</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-olf</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-manuscript</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-legally-suppressed</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-has-pdf</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-has-supplement</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-pdf-only</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-suppress-copyright</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-is-real-version</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-is-scanned-article</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-preprint</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-in-epmc</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-license-ref</meta-name><meta-value>CC BY-NC</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro" disp-level="1" id="s0001"><title>Introduction</title><p>Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), accounting for approximately 90% of all head and neck cancer (HNC) cases, represents a major global health burden, with nearly one million new cases and half a million deaths annually.<xref rid="cit0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> Excessive tobacco use, alcohol consumption, and human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, primarily HPV-16, followed by HPV-18, are generally associated with the development of HNSCC.<xref rid="cit0002" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> Most HNSCC patients are diagnosed with locally advanced stage. Concurrent chemoradiation (CRT) is the standard of care for locally advanced HNSCC (LA-HNSCC). High-dose cisplatin chemotherapy (CT) administered every three weeks, combined with concurrent radiotherapy (RT), is the preferred regimen.<xref rid="cit0003" ref-type="bibr"><sup>3</sup></xref> However, even patients receiving standard CRT often experience local recurrence or distant metastasis.</p><p>The use of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in combination with CRT has emerged as a potential therapeutic option to improve the prognosis of LA-HNSCC.<xref rid="cit0004" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> Unlike other cancers, such as non-small cell lung cancer, in which a sequential combination of CRT plus anti-PD-L1 therapy has become the standard of care for locally advanced stages,<xref rid="cit0005" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> attempts to improve outcomes in LA-HNSCC by adding ICIs have failed to show a survival benefit over CRT alone.<xref rid="cit0006" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> Several immune-related factors may explain these negative results: CRT-induced lymphopenia and myeloid reprogramming can suppress antitumor immunity; treatment-related inflammation may also hinder the effective adaptive immune responses need for ICIs activity.<xref rid="cit0008" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8–10</sup></xref> In fact, the integrated immunologic effects of CRT remain poorly understood, particularly in patients with LA-HNSCC, highlighting the need to better understand the immunologic effects of CRT in this context.</p><p>Our previous findings in cancer patients suggested that platinum-based CRT induced a potent Th1-polarized immune response in both the blood and tumor microenvironment (TME), while upregulating compensatory immunosuppressive cells, such as regulatory T cells (Treg) and myeloid-derived suppressive cells (MDSC).<xref rid="cit0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0012" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> We also demonstrated in mouse tumor models that ICIs, when combined sequentially, can sustain the CRT-induced antitumor T cells, which contributed to optimal tumor control.<xref rid="cit0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> Thus, we assume that CRT could shape anti-tumor T cell responses in some patients with LA-HNSCC, influencing the response to ICIs.</p><p>Here, we investigated the immunomodulatory effects of platinum-based CRT in newly diagnosed patients with LA-HNSCC. Longitudinal and multidimensional analysis of systemic immunity showed that CRT created pro-inflammatory and immunosuppressive environments that shape the dynamics of adaptive T cell responses in the blood. Notably, CRT stimulates delayed and robust expansion of tumor-reactive T cells, which is positively associated with objective response. Our results support the sequential combination of CRT with ICIs, rather than concurrent, in patients with LA-HNSCC.</p></sec><sec disp-level="1" id="s0002"><title>Materials and methods</title><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2001"><title>Patients and study design</title><p>This study, which was registered at <uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://ClinicalTrials.gov">ClinicalTrials.gov</uri> (registration number: <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="pmc:clinical-trial" xlink:href="NCT03117946">NCT03117946</ext-link>), started in June 2017 and ended in January 2019. It was a prospective multicenter observational cohort that aimed to study the systemic immune effects induced by CRT in cancer patients. Forty-eight patients with LA-HNSCC who were histologically confirmed and treated with first-line platinum-based concomitant CRT were included in four cancer centers in France. In this cohort, most patients, 91.7% (44/48), received high-dose cisplatin administered every three weeks (100 mg/m² on days 1, 22, and 43) concurrently with radiotherapy, 2.1% (1/48) of patients were treated with weekly cisplatin at 40 mg/m², while three patients received alternative platinum-based regimens due to clinical considerations. All patients in the study were included after writing the informed consent under French laws. The exclusion criteria included sequential CRT, exclusive or stereotactic RT, prior adjuvant CRT, ongoing immunotherapy, CT, or immunosuppressive treatment (except prednisone or prednisolone ≤ 10 mg/d), human immunodeficiency viruses (HIV) or hepatitis B/C infection, significant medical or psychiatric comorbidities, and legal protection status. HPV infection status was performed by p16 immunohistochemistry in HNSCC patients.<xref rid="cit0013" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref></p><p>Blood samples, peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) and sera were collected at three time points: before the start of treatment (baseline, BSL), one month after the beginning of CRT (CRT-1M), and three months after the beginning of CRT (CRT-3M), which is one month after the end of CRT. PBMCs were isolated using density gradient centrifugation with the lymphocyte separation milieu (Cat. No. CMSMSL01-01, Eurobio, Paris, France). The samples were then stored in liquid nitrogen until they were used.</p><p>CT-scan recordings performed during the follow-up were used to assess the clinical response based on the RECIST version 1.1 criteria. Patients who displayed a complete or partial response were considered responders. Otherwise, they were considered non-responders. Information about patients' outcomes, progression-free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS) were collected up to 4 y after their inclusion.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2002"><title>Synthetic peptides</title><p>A mixture of MHC class II binding peptides derived from tumor-associated antigens (TAAs), such as NY-ESO-1 and telomerase (TERT), was purchased from JPT (Berlin, Germany) were used to assess tumor-specific T cell responses as previously reported.<xref rid="cit0014" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14–16</sup></xref> To assess anti-viral T cell responses, peptide mixtures derived from cytomegalovirus, Epstein–Barr virus, and influenza virus (CEF) were used (Cat. No. PA-CEF-001, Cellular Technology Ltd., Shaker Heights, OH, USA).</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2003"><title>Assessment of tumor-reactive T cell response by IFN-γ ELISpot assay</title><p>Tumor-reactive T cell responses were assessed by IFN-γ ELISpot assay after a short <italic toggle="yes">in vitro</italic> stimulation (IVS) as described previously.<xref rid="cit0012" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0014" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0017" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> Briefly, PBMC were defrosted and then stimulated <italic toggle="yes">in vitro</italic> at 1 × 10<sup>6</sup> cells/well for 6 d in 48-well plates either with mixtures of peptides derived from TERT (5 µg/mL) or NY-ESO-1 (1 µg/mL). Recombinant interleukin 7 (IL-7) (PeproTech, Cranbury, NJ, USA) with 5 ng/mL and 20 UI/mL IL-2 (Novartis, Basel, Switzerland) were added on the 1st and 3rd days, respectively. For anti-virus T cells assessment, PBMC were stimulated at the same time with a mixture of CEF peptides using above protocol. The tumor-specific T cells were measured by Human IFN-γ ELISpot (Cat. No. 856.051.020P, Medix Biochemica, Espoo, Finland) according to the manufacturer's instructions. Briefly, cells were recovered after IVS and seeded at 1 × 10<sup>5</sup> cells/well in X-VIVO 15 medium (Cat. No. 02-053Q, Lonza, Basel, Switzerland) in a 96-well ELISpot plate with the relevant tumor antigens or virus-derived peptides for 16 h. The cells cultured with X-VIVO 15 medium were used as the negative control and cultured with the mix of phorbol 12-myristate 13-acetate (PMA) (1 ng/mL, Cat. No. P8139, Sigma-Aldrich, Darmstadt, Germany) and ionomycin (500 ng/mL, Cat. No. I0634, Sigma-Aldrich) as the positive control. Then, the spot-forming cells were revealed according to the manufacturer's instructions and were counted by the C.T.L. Immunospot reader system (Cellular Technology Ltd.). The response was considered positive if the number of IFN-γ spots was more than twice that of the medium control and exceeded 10.<xref rid="cit0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref></p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2004"><title>Flow cytometry</title><p>Pre- and post-CRT PBMCs were analyzed using multiple flow cytometry panels to assess T cell differentiation, exhaustion, cytokine polarization, Treg, and monocytic myeloid-derived suppressor cells (M-MDSCs). A complete list of monoclonal antibodies with details used for immune cell characterization is provided in Supplementary Table 1.</p><p>Thawed PBMCs were incubated with the fixable viability dye (eBioscience, San Diego, CA, USA) for 10 min at 4 °C, followed by surface staining with monoclonal antibodies for 20–30 min at 4 °C. Treg staining used the DURAClone IM Treg panel (Cat. No. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="pmc:entrez-nucleotide" xlink:href="B53346">B53346</ext-link>, Beckman Coulter, Brea, CA, USA). Intracellular FoxP3 staining, the samples were fixed and permeabilized by the FoxP3/transcription factor buffer set (Cat. No. 00-5523-00, eBioscience). To assess the function of T cells, PBMCs were cultured in X-VIVO 15 and stimulated with PMA/ionomycin in the presence of the intracellular trafficking inhibitor BD GolgiPlug™ (Cat. No. 512301KZ, BD Biosciences, Franklin Lakes, NJ, USA) for 4 h. After surface staining, the cells were permeabilized with BD Perm/Wash buffer (Cat. No. 554723, BD Biosciences) and stained intracellularly for IFN-γ, TNF-α, and IL-2. Data were acquired on the DxFlex flow cytometer (Beckman Coulter) and then analyzed by Kaluza™ software (Beckman Coulter). The gating strategies of the different panels were shown in Supplementary Figure 1.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2005"><title>Cytokines measurement</title><p>Blood cytokines quantification within serum samples was undertaken following the manufacturer's protocols using the following commercially available cytokine bead arrays: LEGENDplex™ Human Th Cytokine Panel (12-plex) (Cat. No. 741027, BioLegend, San Diego, CA, USA) and LEGENDplex™ Human Inflammation Panel 1 (13-plex) (Cat. No. 740808, BioLegend). The data were acquired by the CytoFlex flow cytometer (Beckman Coulter) and analyzed with LEGENDplex™ Data Analysis Software (BioLegend). All the results are shown in pg/mL.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2006"><title>RNA extraction and qRT-PCR analysis</title><p>The extraction of total RNA from PBMC was performed by NucleoSpin® RNA isolation kit (Cat. No. 740990.50, Macherey‐Nagel, Düren, Germany) and reverse transcribed into complementary DNA (cDNA) using PrimeScript™ RT Reagent Kit with gDNA Eraser (Perfect Real Time) (Cat. No. RR047Q, Takara Bio, Kusatsu, Japan) according to the instructions. The NanoDrop 2000 spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to detect the concentration and purity of the total RNA. The RNA purity requires an <italic toggle="yes">A</italic><sub>260</sub>/<italic toggle="yes">A</italic><sub>280</sub> ratio in the range of 1.8 to 2.1. The expression level of genes was explored by quantitative PCR using SYBR® Premix Ex Taq II (Tli RNaseH Plus) (Cat. No. RR820A, Takara Bio) with the CFX96 Real-Time PCR Detection System (Bio-Rad, Hercules, CA, USA). Human <italic toggle="yes">ACTB</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">B2M</italic> were acting as reference genes. For PCR, primers were used, and their sequences are shown in Supplementary Table 2.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2007"><title>Bulk RNA-sequencing</title><p>Bulk RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq) analysis was performed on the BSL, CRT-1M and CRT-3M PBMC samples from 11 patients. Three to five million PBMC per sample were thawed, and RNA was extracted using the Maxwell® CSC RNA Blood Kit (Cat. No. 1S1410, Promega, France) according to the supplier's recommendations and then isolated on columns. Quantification was performed using NanoDrop (Thermo Fisher), and quality control confirmed a RNA integrity score (RIN) greater than seven for all the samples required for sequencing. Library preparation and sequencing benefited from the equipment and services of the iGenSeq core facility at the ICM. Libraries were generated from total RNA using the TruSeq Stranded mRNA LT Kit (Cat. No. RS-122-2101, Illumina). The quality and quantity of the final libraries were assessed using the TapeStation system (Agilent Technologies, Inc.) and the QuantiFluor dsDNA system (Promega Corp.). Paired-end sequencing (2 × 150 bp) was performed on the NovaSeq6000 platform (Illumina) using the NovaSeq 6000 S4 Reagent Kit v1.5 (200 cycles).</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0002-s2008"><title>Bioinformatic analysis</title><p>Bulk RNA-Seq reads were aligned using STAR v2.7.9a to GENCODE Human Release 39 (GRCh38.p13), and the read counts matrix was produced with FeatureCounts v2.0.1, both with default parameters.<xref rid="cit0019" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0020" ref-type="bibr"><sup>20</sup></xref> The bioconductor RNA-seq workflow was followed to detect differential expression genes with DESEQ2 v1.34.0. A total of 19,258 coding genes filtered with *biomaRt* v2.5.3 were analyzed for transcript abundance, and poorly expressed genes were eliminated based on the criteria of a maximum read count &gt; 10 for all samples.<xref rid="cit0021" ref-type="bibr"><sup>21</sup></xref> Data visualization was achieved in R v4.1.1 with the *ComplexHeatmap* package v2.11.1 and the *EnhancedVolcanoPlot* v1.12 using DESeq 2's regularized-logarithm transformation (rlog) of the count data. Genes were considered as differentially expressed genes (DEGs) if they achieved a false discovery rate of 5% or less and a log2-fold change of 0.5 (−0.5 &lt; log2Foldchange &gt; 0.5). Gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) was performed using clusterProfiler (v4.0). Briefly, all statistically enriched terms were identified (GO terms, canonical pathways), and then, accumulative hypergeometric <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>-values and <italic toggle="yes">q</italic>-values were calculated using the Benjamini‒Hochberg procedure to account for multiple testing, and enrichment factors were calculated and used for filtering. The functional relationships among genes within or between GO terms (the “gene-concept network”) were inferred and visualized using the enrichplot package in R v4.1.1. Xcell was used with the 64 immune and stroma cell types of the signature to perform cell type enrichment from the expression data. TCRs were extracted from the RNA-seq data and processed using the tool MIXCR v3.0.13 with the default parameter “analyze shotgun.”<xref rid="cit0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0023" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref> The upstream analysis output was used as input for the *immunarch* v.0.9 package. Basic exploratory analysis was performed by the repExplore function with the volume method, which compares the number of clonotypes. TCR clonality was evaluated using the repClonality function with the homeo method, which assesses clonal space homeostasis. Diversity evaluation was done by the repDiversity function with the d50 method. Computations were performed using the supercomputer facilities of the Mésocentre de calcul de Franche Comté.</p></sec></sec><sec disp-level="1" id="s0003"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p>The normality distribution of the data was checked using the Shapiro‒Wilk normality test. Depending on the normality of the data, the group number to be compared, and the data source, adapted statistical tests were used. Hierarchical cluster analysis was performed, and dendrograms were constructed using online Morpheus software with robust <italic toggle="yes">Z</italic>-score normalization (<uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="https://software.broadinstitute.org/morpheus/">https://software.broadinstitute.org/morpheus/</uri>). For survival analysis, OS and PFS were estimated using the Kaplan‒Meier method, described using rates at specific time points with 95% confidence intervals. Patients known to be alive were censored at the time of their last follow-up assessment. R software (4.3.1) was used for bioinformatic analysis. All analyses except bioinformatic analysis were performed using Prism 8 GraphPad™ software (GraphPad Software Inc., San Diego, CA, USA). <italic toggle="yes">p-</italic>values lower than 0.05 were considered statistically significant (*<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05, **<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01, ***<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.001, and ****<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.0001).</p></sec><sec sec-type="results" disp-level="1" id="s0004"><title>Results</title><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2001"><title>Platinum-based CRT induced a delayed expansion of tumor-reactive T cells in peripheral blood</title><p>From June 2017 to January 2019, 48 patients with LA-HNSCC treated with platinum-based CRT were enrolled, and blood samples were collected at the indicated timepoints for multidimensional immune analysis (<xref rid="f0001" ref-type="fig">Figure 1A</xref>). The main clinical characteristics, including patient outcomes, are summarized in Supplementary Table 3. The median of OS and PFS in all HNSCC patients recruited in this cohort was 45 and 43 months, respectively, which was in concordance with previous data,<xref rid="cit0024" ref-type="bibr"><sup>24</sup></xref> showing that there was no bias in this cohort.</p><fig position="float" id="f0001" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 1.</label><caption><p>Platinum-based chemoradiation induced a delayed expansion of tumor-reactive T cells in peripheral blood. PBMC from HNSCC patients were stimulated with TERT and NY-ESO-1 peptides, and then the IFN-γ ELISpot assay was performed. (A) Scheme of the experiment for HNSCC patients treated with CRT. (B) Kinetics of the frequency of IFN-γ-secreting anti-TERT T cells related to 10<sup>5</sup> T cells (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 43; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 43; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34). On the right panel, mean and SEM were shown. (C) The frequency of changes in patients' responses to the TERT antigen at different time points (increase/sustain: continued positivity or change from negative to positive; decrease: change from positive to negative; negative: continued negativity). (D) Kinetics of the frequency of IFN-γ-secreting anti-NY-ESO-1 T cells related to 10<sup>5</sup> T cells (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 35; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 35; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 27). On the right panel, mean and SEM were shown. (E) The frequency of changes in patients' responses to NY-ESO-1 antigen at different time points (increase/sustain: continued positivity or change from negative to positive; decrease: change from positive to negative; negative: continued negativity). (F) Distribution of patients according to their positive or negative IFN-γ T cell responses against 0, 1, or 2 antigens (percentages of negative and positive patients was shown). ns: not significant, *<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05, **<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01, and ***<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.001.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0001_C.jpg"/></fig><p>The circulating T cell responses against TERT and NY-ESO-1 were assessed in 43 LA-HNSCC patients with available samples at all three timepoints. The anti-TERT T cell response was detected in 18/43 (41.9%) at BSL, and this rate decreased to 9/43 (20.9%) at CRT-1M and then reached 76.5% (26/34) at CRT-3M (<xref rid="f0001" ref-type="fig">Figure 1B</xref>). At CRT-1M, 18.6% of patients showed an increased or sustained anti-TERT T-cell response, while 32.6% and 48.8% showed a decrease or no change (remained negative), respectively. In contrast, 3 months after CRT, an expansion of the anti-TERT T-cell response in the blood was found in around 70% of patients compared with CRT-1M or BSL (<xref rid="f0001" ref-type="fig">Figure 1C</xref>). Similar kinetics were shown for the circulating NY-ESO-1-specific T cell response as well as in the group of patients exhibiting T cell responses directed against both TERT and NY-ESO-1 (<xref rid="f0001" ref-type="fig">Figure 1D–F</xref>). The main clinical characteristics are well balanced between patients with or without anti-tumor T cell response at BSL (Supplementary Table 4). Analyses restricted to early-stage patients (stage I–II) and to locally advanced (stage III–IV) patients showed anti-tumor T cell response kinetics comparable to those observed in the full cohort (Supplementary Figures 2 and 3).</p><p>To determine whether these kinetics were specific to tumor-specific responses or common across other antigenic responses, we measured the anti-viral responses by IFN-γ ELISpot. Among the 26 patients tested, 22 patients (84.6%) had an anti-viral T cell response at BSL, while 18 patients (69.2%) had an anti-viral response after CRT (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.324) (Supplementary Figure 4A and B). No significant differences in the frequencies of anti-viral T cell responses were found between patients with or without tumor-specific T cell responses at BSL and those after CRT (Supplementary Figure 4C and D). This suggests that CRT action was preferentially directed against antigen-specific T cell responses.</p><p>To investigate whether the transient decrease of at CRT-1M was not due to T cell dysfunction, we performed non-antigen-specific polyclonal T cell stimulation with PMA-ionomycin. We found no difference in the ability of CD4<sup>+</sup> or CD8<sup>+</sup> T cells to secrete IFN-γ, regardless of the anti-tumor T cell response status at CRT-1M (Supplementary Figure 4E and F). As expected, HPV infection status did not influence the dynamics of tumor-reactive T cell responses measured in blood (Supplementary Figure 4G and H). Thus, CRT promotes a delayed expansion of functional tumor-reactive T cells in the blood.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2002"><title>CRT promotes the expansion of polyfunctional effector memory T cells in the blood</title><p>Because the ELISpot assay did not allow a precise analysis of the effect of CRT on the phenotype and function of circulating T cells, we further assessed T cell function and phenotype by flow cytometry. First, we assessed the ability of CRT to induce cytokine polyfunctional T cells expansion in the blood. Cytokine polyfunctional T cells (polyF T cells) are described as high-quality T cells that produce multiple Th1 cytokines (IFN-γ, IL-2, and TNF-α) concurrently.<xref rid="cit0025" ref-type="bibr"><sup>25</sup></xref> To this end, PBMCs were stimulated with PMA/ionomycin for 4 h, and cytokine polyF T cells were measured by flow cytometry (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2A</xref> and <xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">B</xref>). Results showed that polyF T cells co-secreting IFN-γ, IL-2, and TNF-α were induced early at CRT-1M within both the CD4<sup>+</sup> and CD8<sup>+</sup> subsets and significantly increased in most patients at CRT-3M (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2C</xref> and <xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">D</xref>; Supplementary Figure 5A and B). We observed that 90.9% of patients with polyF T cells exhibited a CRT-expanded anti-tumor specific T cell response (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2E</xref>). Next, we studied the effect of CRT on the circulating T cells differentiation profile (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2F</xref>). Compared to BSL, increased level of effector memory T cells (T<sub>EM</sub> cells) subset was found <italic toggle="yes">ex vivo</italic> in blood after CRT in most patients (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2G–J</xref>). Most patients with CRT-induced antitumor T cells had circulating T cells with T<sub>EM</sub> cells (<xref rid="f0002" ref-type="fig">Figure 2K</xref>).</p><fig position="float" id="f0002" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 2.</label><caption><p>Chemoradiation induces polyfunctional T cells and effector memory T cells related to anti-TAA-specific T cell responses. (A) PBMCs from HNSCC patients were stimulated with PMA-ionomycin, and then, intracellular staining was performed. (B) Representative plots from a patient at three time points (in IL-2 plots, numbers without parentheses indicate parent percentages; in parentheses, grandparent percentages). (C) Co-expression rate of IFN-γ, IL-2 and TFN-α in CD4<sup>+</sup> T cells at BSL, CRT-1M and CRT-3M in HNSCC patients (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34). Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. (D) Kinetics of IFN-γ<sup>+</sup> IL-2<sup>+</sup> TNF-α<sup>+</sup> CD4<sup>+</sup> T cells at different time points (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34). Median was shown. (E) Pie chart showing the frequency of anti-tumor specific T cell responses in patients with increasing levels of polyfunctional T cells at CRT-3M (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 33). (F) Representative plots of a patient at three time points in T cell differentiation. Mean and SEM were shown. (G) Bar plots showed time-dependent distribution of CD45RA<sup>+</sup> CCR7<sup>+</sup> naïve, CD45RA<sup>−</sup> CCR7<sup>+</sup> central memory, CD45RA<sup>−</sup> CCR7<sup>−</sup> effector, and CD45RA<sup>+</sup> CCR7<sup>−</sup> TEMRA cells in CD4<sup>+</sup> T cell populations in HNSCC patients (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20). Mean and SEM were shown. (H) Bar plots showed time-dependent distribution of CD45RA<sup>+</sup> CCR7<sup>+</sup> naïve, CD45RA<sup>−</sup> CCR7<sup>+</sup> central memory, CD45RA<sup>−</sup> CCR7<sup>−</sup> effector, and CD45RA<sup>+</sup> CCR7<sup>−</sup> TEMRA cells in CD8<sup>+</sup> T cell populations in HNSCC patients. (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20). Mean and SEM were shown. (I) Kinetics of CD4<sup>+</sup> T<sub>EM</sub> at different time points (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20). Mean was shown. (J) Kinetics of CD8<sup>+</sup> T<sub>EM</sub> at different time points (BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20). Mean was shown. (K) Pie chart showing the frequency of anti-tumor specific T cell responses in patients with the increase of CD4<sup>+</sup> T<sub>EM</sub> at CRT-3M (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13). ns: not significant, **<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01, ***<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.001, and ***<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.0001.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0002_C.jpg"/></fig></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2003"><title>Upregulation of pathways related to T cell-mediated immunity after CRT</title><p>To explore the factors that influence tumor-specific T cell responses during CRT, we performed bulk RNA-seq of PBMCs from 11 HNSCC patients undergoing CRT. The patients included in the bulk RNA-seq analysis were selected according to the presence or absence of anti-tumor T cell response detected by the ELISpot assay, and sample availability, as well as RNA quality. The main clinical characteristics of those patients selected for RNA-Seq are consistent with those of the whole study population (Supplementary Table 5). The Venn diagram showed 60.7% of the DEGs are down-regulated during treatment, and 17.9% and 17.7% are exclusively up-regulated at CRT-1M and CRT-3M, respectively. Overall, 3.8% of the DEGs are up-regulated in both CRT-1M and CRT-3M (<xref rid="f0003" ref-type="fig">Figures 3A, B</xref> and <xref rid="f0004" ref-type="fig">4A</xref>).</p><fig position="float" id="f0003" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 3.</label><caption><p>Transcriptome changes between CRT-1M and BSL. (A) Venn diagram showing the percentage and number of differentially expressed genes at different time points. (B) Heatmap showing all differentially expressed genes meeting the criterion of |log2foldchange| &gt; 0.5 and padj &lt; 0.05 between CRT-1M and BSL. (C) Gene set enrichment analysis by clusterProfiler 4.0 highlights pathways enriched at CRT-1M (in comparison with BSL). (D) GSEA plot showed the distribution of selected pathways for CRT-1M vs BSL. (E) In the volcano plot, DEGs related to the immune response between CRT-1M and BSL are shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n </italic>= 11; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n </italic>= 11; CRT-3M, and <italic toggle="yes">n </italic>= 11.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0003_C.jpg"/></fig><fig position="float" id="f0004" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 4.</label><caption><p>Long-lasting transcriptome changes between CRT-3M and BSL. (A) Heatmap showing all differentially expressed genes meeting the criterion of |log2foldchange| &gt; 0.5 and padj &lt; 0.05 between CRT-3M and BSL. (B) Gene set enrichment analysis by clusterProfiler 4.0 highlights pathways enriched at CRT-3M (in comparison with BSL). (C) GSEA plot showed the distribution of selected pathways for CRT-3M vs BSL. (D) DEGs related to the immune response between CRT-3M and BSL are shown in the volcano plot. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 11; CRT-3M, and <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 11.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0004_C.jpg"/></fig><p>At CRT-1M vs BSL, gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) showed that the DEGs were enriched for adaptive T cell pathway alongside the innate/interferon signatures, with coordinated repression of B cell pathways (<xref rid="f0003" ref-type="fig">Figure 3C</xref> and <xref rid="f0003" ref-type="fig">D</xref>; Supplementary Figure 6A). The volcano plot showed key shifts in immune gene expression between CRT-1M and BSL (<xref rid="f0003" ref-type="fig">Figure 3E</xref>). B cell-associated genes (<italic toggle="yes">FCRL1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">FCRL2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">BTLA</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CD19</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CD22</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">CD79A</italic>) were downregulated, whereas IFN-responsive/innate and trafficking mediators (<italic toggle="yes">IFI6</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GBP1</italic>/<italic toggle="yes">2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">FCGR1A</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">SLAMF8</italic>; <italic toggle="yes">CXCL9/10/11</italic>) and activation markers (<italic toggle="yes">STAT1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">PARP9</italic>, and TNFSF10) were upregulated (<xref rid="f0003" ref-type="fig">Figure 3E</xref>).</p><p>By CRT-3M, the robust effect of CRT during treatment was sustained over time (<xref rid="f0004" ref-type="fig">Figure 4A</xref>). Results showed that B cell pathway remained suppressed and that adaptive T cell immunity was sustained, with persistent pro-inflammatory activity (<italic toggle="yes">TNFRSF1B</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">FCGR1A</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">FCGR2A</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">CXCL10</italic>) and increased dendritic cell differentiation/activation transcripts (<italic toggle="yes">CD86</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CLEC9A, PLSCR1, CD1D</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">CSF2RB</italic>), indicating a favorable milieu to T-cell priming and survival (<xref rid="f0004" ref-type="fig">Figure 4B–D</xref>; Supplementary Figure 6B). Together, we observed a shift from B-cell-driven immunity towards the upregulation of innate and inflammatory responses and T cell-mediated immunity after CRT.</p><p>Transcriptome-based TCR-β analysis revealed a reduction in unique clonotypes and diversity among patients after CRT, indicating oligoclonal expansion (Supplementary Figure 7A–D). Clonal expansion analysis supported this finding, revealing substantial clonal expansion of large clones and a reduction in medium clones after CRT (Supplementary Figure 7E and F). Clonotype tracking further confirmed that the most common clonotypes (top 10) present in the BSL decreased at CRT-3M, while clonotypes not detected at BSL were identified at CRT-3M (Supplementary Figure 7G).</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2004"><title>Biphasic expression pattern of genes related to T-cell function, polarization, and exhaustion after CRT</title><p>To investigate the factors influencing CRT-induced systemic antitumor T cell responses, we further analyzed the expression of immune-related genes in T cells using RT-qPCR. As shown in <xref rid="f0005" ref-type="fig">Figure 5A</xref>, T cell activation-related genes (<italic toggle="yes">ICOS</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CD69</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CD40L</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">OX40</italic>) transiently decreased at CRT-1M before increasing at CRT-3M. Memory-associated genes (<italic toggle="yes">IL7R</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">EOMES</italic>) showed a similar trend. Genes related to Th1, Th2, Th17 and Treg polarization (<italic toggle="yes">TBX21</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GATA3, PRDM1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">RORC and FOXP3)</italic> were downregulated at CRT-1M but significantly upregulated at CRT-3M, and cytotoxicity-related genes (<italic toggle="yes">PRF1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GZMB</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GZMK</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GZMM</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GNLY</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">NKG7</italic>) were markedly decreased early during CRT but recovered at CRT-3M (<xref rid="f0005" ref-type="fig">Figure 5B</xref> and <xref rid="f0005" ref-type="fig">C</xref>). Exhaustion-associated genes such as <italic toggle="yes">TIGIT</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">TIM-3</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">TOX</italic>, PD-1, and <italic toggle="yes">TCF7</italic> also followed a similar dynamic phenomenon (<xref rid="f0005" ref-type="fig">Figure 5D</xref>). We confirmed these exhaustion features at the protein level using flow cytometry and found that CRT promoted the expansion of the exhaustion markers PD-1, TIM-3, and TIGIT over time (Supplementary Figure 8).</p><fig position="float" id="f0005" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 5.</label><caption><p>Chemoradiation induces the dynamic expression of immune-related genes. (A) Expression level of activation and memory-related genes (ICOS, CD69, CD40L, OX40, IL7R, and EOMES) in PBMCs from HNSCC patients collected from BSL, CRT-1M, and CRT-3M. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. (B) Expression of Th polarization genes (FOXP3, TBX21, GATA3, RUNX3, PRDM1, RORC, and BCL6) in PBMCs from HNSCC patients. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. (C) Expression of cytotoxicity-related genes (PRF1, GZMB, GZMK, GZMM, GNLY, and NKG7) in PBMCs from HNSCC patients. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. (D) Expression of immune exhaustion-related genes (TIGIT, TIM3, TOX, PD-1, and TCF-7) in PBMCs from HNSCC patients. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. (E) Heatmap showed the hierarchical clustering of mRNA expression profiles with anti-TERT and anti-NY-ESO-1 T cell responses. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8. *<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05 and **<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0005_C.jpg"/></fig><p>Overall, hierarchical clustering analysis revealed that all these genes related to T cell function, polarization, and exhaustion experienced a transient decrease at CRT-1M, followed by a marked increase at CRT-3M, and patients exhibiting positive tumor-specific T cell responses displayed higher expression levels of these genes (<xref rid="f0005" ref-type="fig">Figure 5E</xref>).</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2005"><title>CRT induces a pro-inflammatory environment and expands circulating immunosuppressive cells</title><p>To assess the changes in the cytokine blood milieu induced by CRT, we performed multiple cytokine detection analyses in the serum collected from patients before and after CRT. The radar chart showed a high degree of variability in the cytokines (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6A</xref>). We observed an initial increase in pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-α at CRT-1M, followed by a decrease at CRT-3M, while IL-33, IL-1β, and IL-8 remained higher over time after CRT (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6B</xref>). Although anti-inflammatory cytokines IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, and IL-22 showed an increase at CRT-1M followed by a decrease at CRT-3M, IL-10 showed sustained elevation at both CRT-1M and CRT-3M as a potential compensatory mechanism (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6C</xref>).</p><fig position="float" id="f0006" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 6.</label><caption><p>Influence of CRT on serum cytokines and immunosuppressive cells in HNSCC patients. (A) Radar chart showed the mean level of each cytokine at different timepoints in HNSCC patients treated with CRT. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 12; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13. (B) The level of IL-33, IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α, and IL-8 in the serum of HNSCC patients treated with CRT. Mean and SEM were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 12; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13. (C) The level of IL-4, IL-13, IL-22, and IL-10 in the serum of HNSCC patients treated with CRT. Mean and SEM were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 12; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 13. (D) Kinetics of Treg at different time points in HNSCC patients. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20. (E) The level of Treg in HNSCC patients treated with CRT. Mean and SEM were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 8; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 20. (F) anti-TERT T cell response/Treg ratio in HNSCC patients at different timepoints. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 16; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 5; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 14. (G) Kinetics of M-MDSCs at different time points in HNSCC patients. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34. (H) The level of M-MDSCs in HNSCC patients treated with CRT. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 48; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34. (I) anti-TERT T cell response/M-MDSC ratio in HNSCC patients at different timepoints. Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. BSL, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 44; CRT-1M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 43; CRT-3M, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 34. ns: not significant, *<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05 and **<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0006_C.jpg"/></fig><p>Next, we evaluated the kinetic of immune suppressive cells Treg and MDSC. There was a significant increase in the proportion of circulating Treg at 3 months compared to baseline (BSL: 2.69% vs. CRT-3M: 4.15%, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.01) (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6D</xref> and <xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">E</xref>). Despite the CRT-induced elevation of Treg, the anti-TERT/Treg ratio remained significantly elevated after treatment (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6F</xref>). Regarding M-MDSC, their proportion also increased at CRT-1M, but the difference was not statistically significant (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6G</xref> and <xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">H</xref>). Moreover, the anti-TERT/M-MDSC ratio significantly increased after CRT in HNSCC patients (<xref rid="f0006" ref-type="fig">Figure 6I</xref>). Thus, the level of anti-tumor T cells induced by CRT seemed stronger than the immunosuppressive cells.</p></sec><sec disp-level="2" id="s0004-s2006"><title>Integrated analysis reveals distinct blood immune signatures following CRT in patients with LA-HNSCC</title><p>To explore the relationships between circulating anti-tumor–specific responses and other immune parameters, we performed principal component analysis (PCA) integrating anti-tumor T cell responses, cytokine profiles, Treg, and M-MDSC (<xref rid="f0007" ref-type="fig">Figure 7A</xref>). The analysis revealed four distinct immune patterns distributed along two principal axes. Tumor-specific T cell responses clustered with pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-6, MCP-1, IFN-γ, and IL-12p70), whereas Th17-associated cytokines (IL-17A and IL-22) formed a separate cluster. M-MDSC were closely associated with chemokines such as IL-8 and IL-1 family members (IL-18 and IL-33), while Treg were grouped with Th2-related cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13).</p><fig position="float" id="f0007" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 7.</label><caption><p>The clinical response to CRT was related to the intensity of anti-tumor immune responses. (A) Principal component analysis showed the relationship among anti-tumor antigen T cell response, Treg, M-MDSC, and cytokines in HNSCC patients. (B) Principal component analysis showing similarity between samples based on gene expression, CRT-3M vs BSL. (C) Deconvolution showed the cell fraction enriched at CRT-3M between responders (R) and non-responders (NR). (D) The intensity of anti-tumor (left), anti-TERT (middle), and anti-NY-ESO-1 (right) T cells related to the response to CRT at CRT-3M (responders (R): <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 19; non-responders (NRs): <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> = 10). Median and 95% confidence interval were shown. ns: not significant, *<italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="color" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_F0007_C.jpg"/></fig><p>According to the PCA based on the integration of RNA-Seq results and clinical response, there were two groups of patients corresponding to responders and non-responders (<xref rid="f0007" ref-type="fig">Figure 7B</xref>). Responders to CRT were more homogeneous than non-responders, and the difference between these two groups was clearer at CRT-3M, supporting the effect of CRT.</p><p>Exploratory analysis using cellular deconvolution showed that responders had a higher proportion of Th1 cells than non-responders, even though they also exhibited a higher proportion of Treg (<xref rid="f0007" ref-type="fig">Figure 7C</xref>). Accordingly, we found higher CRT-induced tumor-reactive T cells, especially against TERT, in responders than in non-responders (<xref rid="f0007" ref-type="fig">Figure 7D</xref>).</p><p>Overall, during the early phase of CRT, there is a decrease in anti-tumor T cell response, and upregulation of immunosuppression, inflammation and exhaustion of T cells, whereas strong expansion of anti-tumor T cells, which counteract immunosuppression and exhaustion, occurred in the later phase after CRT (Supplementary Figure 9).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" disp-level="1" id="s0005"><title>Discussion</title><p>It is widely acknowledged that CRT profoundly affects immune function.<xref rid="cit0026" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> However, no consensus has been reached on how it influences antitumor T cell immunity in LA-HNSCC patients. Resolving this question is crucial in the context of adding immunotherapy. Here, we investigated the systemic immune effects of platinum-based CRT in patients with LA-HNSCC and showed the ability of CRT to generate a blood signature including pro-inflammatory cytokines and strong expansion of tumor reactive T cell responses in a delayed manner that coexist with CRT-induced compensatory immunosuppressive cells expansion. This transient decrease in blood mainly concerned T cells directed against TERT and NY-ESO-1, two shared tumor-associated antigens (TAA) expressed in HNSCC.<xref rid="cit0027" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref> In addition, the antiviral T cell responses measured at the same time were not affected by CRT. This delayed expansion of tumor reactive T cell responses along with Treg and MDSC induction are in line with our previous observations in NSCLC patients treated with platinum-based CRT.<xref rid="cit0012" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> This observation could be explained by the early translocation of tumor-specific circulating T cells to the tumor during CRT.<xref rid="cit0028" ref-type="bibr"><sup>28</sup></xref> Indeed, the results from bulk RNA-seq showed an early upregulation during CRT of chemokines involved in the migration of effector T cells to tumor sites, such as CXCL9, CXCL10, and CXCL11.<xref rid="cit0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref> Access to tumor samples would be required to confirm this assumption. Doescher et al. reported that CRT induces marked transcriptional remodeling of the tumor microenvironment in HNSCC patients, including the modulation of immune-related genes associated with T-cell recruitment and treatment response.<xref rid="cit0030" ref-type="bibr"><sup>30</sup></xref> Notably, our previous report in mouse models showed a large and early influx of effector T cells in the tumor bed after cisplatin-based CRT, a process related to the upregulation of CXCL9 and CXCL10 in the CRT-exposed TME.<xref rid="cit0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> Thus, what appears in blood as a decline or loss of tumor-specific response may in fact reflect active homing of those effectors into the irradiated tumor.</p><p>After the transient decrease in tumor-specific T cells in the blood, we observed robust amplification of these responses at CRT-3M. At this time point, CRT acted as an <italic toggle="yes">in situ</italic> vaccine. The enhanced T cell responses against response at CRT-3M may be attributed to the release of tumor antigens from dying tumor cells induced by CRT and the upregulation of their presentation by antigen-presenting cells.<xref rid="cit0031" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref> Radiation-induced genomic instability leads to the release of double-stranded DNA or RNA into the cytoplasm of dying cells. Cyclic guanosine monophosphate (GMP)-AMP synthase (cGAS) is a key intracellular DNA sensor that activates the downstream stimulator of interferon genes (STING) pathway, triggering nuclear factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling and inducing the expression of interferons and pro-inflammatory cytokines.<xref rid="cit0032" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref> We showed an elevation of NF-κB-dependent proinflammatory cytokines TNF-α, IL-6, and IL-1β in the serum at CRT-1M, which can promote the activation of dendritic cells, thereby further amplifying anti-tumor T cell responses, as previously reported.<xref rid="cit0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref></p><p>This study also revealed contradictory immunological effects of CRT. While there was evidence of immunostimulatory effects, immune-suppressive cells, including Treg and M-MDSCs, also showed varying degrees of expansion. The upregulation of Treg after RT is a well-known phenomenon that may undermine therapeutic efficacy, leading to tumor progression and recurrence. Consistent with this, Schuler et al. found an increased frequency of highly suppressed, cisplatin-resistant Treg in HNSCC patients receiving CRT, which persisted after CRT, potentially contributing to immune escape and recurrence.<xref rid="cit0034" ref-type="bibr"><sup>34</sup></xref> Additionally, transcriptional profiling from CRT-treated glioma patients revealed the enrichment of many myeloid function-associated genes after CRT, including the chemokine-related genes CXCR4 and CCL20.<xref rid="cit0035" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref> Despite CRT inducing Treg and M-MDSC generation, CRT-induced anti-tumor T cells significantly outweighed its immunosuppressive consequences of CRT in our study. The use of cisplatin in the CRT regimen may also reduce the expansion of MDSC and Treg.<xref rid="cit0036" ref-type="bibr"><sup>36</sup></xref> In addition, we previously demonstrated an opposite relationship between the TERT-reactive T cell response and MDSC in cancer patients.<xref rid="cit0037" ref-type="bibr"><sup>37</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0038" ref-type="bibr"><sup>38</sup></xref></p><p>Interestingly, the kinetics of systemic T cell immunity observed in CRT-treated LA-HNSCC patients explain findings in the clinic. During the early phase of CRT, when concurrent ICIs have been added in clinical trials, we observed not only the defect of tumor-specific T cell responses but also the lowest ratio of anti-TERT T cell responses to immunosuppressive cells. This indicated that tumor-specific T cell responses were constrained by immunosuppressive mechanisms in the early phase of CRT, a window in which adding ICIs may be biologically mistimed and potentially neutralized by compensatory immunosuppression. Such an unfavorable immune context may explain why previous large, randomized trials evaluating concurrent ICIs with CRT failed to improve the clinical outcomes of patients with LA-HNSCC.<xref rid="cit0006" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> Additionally, the use of anti-PD-1 agents during CRT could dampen anti-tumor immunity, potentially due to the increased sensitivity of CD8<sup>+</sup> T cells to RT and subsequent cell death.<xref rid="cit0039" ref-type="bibr"><sup>39</sup></xref></p><p>Despite recent data from KEYNOTE-412 showing an improvement in event-free survival (EFS) with concurrent and adjuvant pembrolizumab,<xref rid="cit0040" ref-type="bibr"><sup>40</sup></xref> our findings suggested that the benefit may emerge only when ICIs coincide with the recovery of tumor-specific T cell responses following the early immunosuppressive phase. This is particularly plausible since the improvement in EFS might come from the adjuvant part of pembrolizumab (14 injections), which was much longer than the concurrent part (2 injections). The high expansion of anti-tumor T cell response we observed at one month after the end of CRT (CRT-3M), compared to immunosuppressive cells, provided a mechanistic rationale that ICIs could be used as monotherapy after CRT completion as truly adjuvant therapy. The use of adjuvant durvalumab therapy within one month post-CRT for 1 y in stage III NSCLC patients supported our assumption.<xref rid="cit0041" ref-type="bibr"><sup>41</sup></xref> The success of recent clinical trials, which demonstrated that perioperative or postoperative use of anti-PD-1 before and after CRT resulted in significant clinical improvement, also supports our observations.<xref rid="cit0042" ref-type="bibr"><sup>42</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0043" ref-type="bibr"><sup>43</sup></xref></p><p>A concurrent combination strategy of ICIs and CRT remains conceivable. Our findings, together with those of prior work, indicated that concurrent ICIs monotherapy may be insufficient unless paired with agents that counteract CRT-induced immunosuppression. In mouse models, we previously demonstrated that cisplatin-based CRT induces highly suppressive CTLA-4<sup>+</sup> Treg within the tumor microenvironment and that dual CTLA-4 and PD-1 blockade given concurrently with CRT achieved superior tumor control compared with concurrent CRT plus anti-PD-1 alone.<xref rid="cit0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> These data suggested that the concurrent use of ICIs and CRT may require the combination of agents targeting Tregs, MDSCs, or other immunosuppressive mechanisms induced by CRT, while further validation is needed.</p><p>One of the limitations of this study is the lack of analysis of tumor expression of target antigens. Although we were not able to validate TERT and NY-ESO-1 expression in the corresponding tumor samples, the overexpression of TERT is commonly described in 75%–100% of HNSCC tumor tissues. But this is not the case with NY-ESO-1 expression, which is less described in this tumor.<xref rid="cit0044" ref-type="bibr"><sup>44</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0045" ref-type="bibr"><sup>45</sup></xref> Nevertheless, the kinetics of NY-ESO-1-specific T-cell responses after CRT were exactly the same as those specific to TERT, in contrast to antiviral T-cell responses. This suggests that these two shared tumor antigens would be expressed in patients' tumors in this cohort.</p><p>Overall, evidence supports the involvement of systemic adaptive immunity in cancer immunosurveillance and the response to ICIs.<xref rid="cit0046" ref-type="bibr"><sup>46–48</sup></xref> We previously reported that the pre-existing or treatment-induced circulating anti-TERT Th1 response is associated with improved clinical benefit to ICIs.<xref rid="cit0049" ref-type="bibr"><sup>49</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="cit0050" ref-type="bibr"><sup>50</sup></xref> Thus, the ability of CRT to induce a pro-inflammatory blood signature and delayed amplification of circulating antitumor T cell immunity established a good immune environment for sequential ICIs combination with cisplatin-based CRT in LA-HNSCC.</p><p>In addition to systemic immune monitoring, further validation in appropriate immunocompetent <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic> models is needed to formally test the hypothesis that treatment sequencing and combination strategies influence tumor control. Syngeneic HNSCC models provide a platform in which platinum-based CRT could be combined with ICIs initiated concurrently with CRT or after CRT completion as adjuvant therapy. The kinetics of immune changes in the TME could provide a rationale for determining the optimal timing of immunotherapy administration. Such studies would directly address whether sequential CRT followed by immunotherapy truly leads to improved tumor control and would help refine the design of future biomarker-informed clinical trials in HNSCC and other cancers.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Material</title><supplementary-material id="SM7857" position="float" content-type="local-data" orientation="portrait"><caption><title>Supplementary material</title><p>Supplementary materials_Clean version_V2.docx</p></caption><media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="KONI_A_2635806_SM7857.docx" position="float" orientation="portrait"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><ack><title>Acknowledgment</title><p>We thank all the patients and workers from the Oncologic and Radiotherapy Departments of the Georges François Leclerc Center, the North Franche-Comté Hospital, the Jean Godinot Institute, and the University Hospital of Besançon. We also thank the Biomonitoring Platform of the UMR Right Laboratory. The authors declared using generative artificial intelligence technology ChatGPT-5 for English proofreading during the writing of this article.</p></ack><sec sec-type="supplementary-material" disp-level="1" id="s0006"><title>Supplemental material</title><p>Supplemental data for this article can be accessed at <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.1080/2162402X.2026.2635806" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.1080/2162402X.2026.2635806</ext-link>.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" disp-level="1" id="s0007"><title>Disclosure of potential conflicts of interest</title><p>No potential conflicts of interest were disclosed.</p></sec><sec sec-type="d